energy-balance-3e436630.interpretation:
The formula for the Bonnor-Ebert mass is given by 
(M_{BE} = 1.18 \frac{c_s^3}{
sqrt{G^3 \rho}}
). Here, 
(c_s
) is the isothermal sound speed within the gas, representing the speed at which pressure waves propagate and thus a measure of the internal thermal pressure. A higher sound speed implies greater internal pressure, which can support a more massive cloud. 
(G
) is the gravitational constant, quantifying the strength of gravitational attraction. A stronger gravitational constant would lead to a smaller maximum stable mass. 
(\rho
) is the initial density of the gas at the center of the cloud. Higher density means more mass in a given volume, and thus a greater tendency to collapse under gravity, leading to a smaller Bonnor-Ebert mass. The factor 1.18 is a dimensionless constant derived from the detailed mathematical solution of the Lane-Emden equation for isothermal spheres.

energy-balance-3e436630.limits_and_boundary:
As the external pressure approaches zero, the Bonnor-Ebert mass approaches infinity, meaning arbitrarily massive clouds could be stable in a vacuum. Conversely, as the density 
(\rho
) approaches zero (for a fixed sound speed 
(c_s
) and gravity 
(G
)), the Bonnor-Ebert mass approaches infinity. As the isothermal sound speed 
(c_s
) approaches zero (i.e., a very cold gas where thermal pressure is negligible), the Bonnor-Ebert mass approaches zero, indicating that even a small amount of mass will collapse under gravity.

energy-balance-3e436630.conceptual_definition:
The Bonnor-Ebert mass, denoted as 
(M_{BE}
), represents the maximum mass a self-gravitating, isothermal gas sphere can have before it becomes unstable and collapses under its own gravity. It arises from a balance between the internal thermal pressure, which tries to expand the sphere, and the external pressure, typically from the surrounding interstellar medium, which confines it, along with gravity trying to collapse it. Specifically, this formula, derived for a spherically symmetric, isothermal gas cloud, identifies the critical mass at which the inward pull of gravity overcomes the outward push of thermal pressure and external confinement.

